your  gig booking going  um it's not with the way restrictions  are coming and going  i'm finding out two days beforehand oh  by the way you're not working thursday  like many pennsylvanians chris collected  an additional 600  a week in unemployment benefits through  the federal pandemic unemployment  assistance program  but those checks stopped in july even  though he believes he's eligible for  additional aid  he's had trouble filing claims online  the pennsylvania department of labor  said that it can't comment on  individual cases but that it notifies  people when the status of their claims  change and when payments are stopped or  held for various reasons  now that his savings have dwindled chris  is worried he won't have enough to pay  the mortgage for the new house he bought  at the beginning of the year  just looking at the bank account and  realizing all right when i pay  a b and c i got x left over that's gotta  that's gotta be it there's no known  income for right now and  what few things i do have in the works  before the end of the month  i can't guarantee they're going to  happen chris stopped paying liability  insurance earlier this year  and he says he uses small hacks to save  money like  getting groceries from a local  non-profit that distributes produce  he hopes to receive more stimulus money  and that a vaccine can help the  restaurant and entertainment industries  make a comeback  yet his anxiety continues to grow  can you see the light at the end of the  tunnel i want to  i do want to because that's who i am as  a person but it's it's that that  i feel like it's getting farther away  [Music]  chris gets his inexpensive produce from  jessica rights  she and her husband nick freeman are  catering professionals who  like many americans chose to pivot after  being laid off from their full-time jobs  what began as a mission to help their  neighbors get produce at the beginning  of the pandemic  has turned into a full-blown non-profit  known as mount airy groceries  which delivers low cost and in most  cases free boxes of food to people all  over the city with the help of other  organizations  tell me about the need what types of  people are you serving there's a wide  range of people that we serve there are  people that are  newly food insecure because of kovid  there are people that are at home  who are afraid to go the grocery store  because they're chronically ill  i will say that probably the largest  population of people that we serve  however  are mothers at home with school aged  children they say their goal is to  continue growing and serving their  community  but they like many of their neighbors  are feeling the stress of not having  stable jobs  i heard coronavirus one day and then  like  less than two weeks later i was out of a  job and i think that was the case for  millions of people in this country  nick received some unemployment benefits  early in the pandemic and has since  taken a part-time job  but nine months in with foster kids at  home and bills that are piling up they  say they might have to make  cuts like selling a car and a cabin they  bought earlier in the year  everything now we are very very  carefully going through what's necessary  or not jessica and nick are now trying  to find a way to structure salaries into  their non-profit  without a paycheck jessica says she'll  have to take a job elsewhere  something she no longer wants to do  especially now that she's seen the food  insecurity in her community  where are your covet anxiety levels i  think people can take a little bit of  anxiety you have that flight or fight  response  long term sustained  worry and stress at some point  there's like a there's a breaking point  and unfortunately i think for many of us  we're right about there i didn't expect  this to happen everything is pretty shut  down i worry about my neighbors i'm  worried about my friends  dana profit has been helping jessica and  nick with their mount airy grocery's  website  a copywriter who was laid off from her  job of 10 years in august  she's been taking on side projects to  enhance her portfolio  but applying for jobs at age 49 is  something she never  imagined she'd have to do what is it  like to job search  after being at a company for so long  it's  difficult it i had to relearn a lot of  things  every rejection or none response that i  receive  is depressing i get i get blue is it  hard to not take it personally i always  take it personally you do  how can you not want me i'm fabulous  dana started taking online classes in  graphic design so she can  ultimately become a one-stop shop for  small businesses looking to start a  website  so far she's relied on severance and  unemployment benefits to get by  but if she doesn't get additional  stimulus money from washington  she's worried about covering big  expenses like health insurance  especially in the middle of a pandemic  rent worries her too  though she says she's not getting  evicted because her sister is her  landlord  you're in a position like many americans  where you are lucky to have a family  yes but you don't you've said you don't  want to lean on them too much  no why i don't want to drain their  finances to help me  so i rather for us just to  hold each other up rather than me  climbing on their shoulders  to survive there's people to help me  but i'm going to try to save pull myself  up first  but how long it will take mount airy's  residents and americans across the  country  to pull themselves up is still unknown  despite a vaccine rollout across the u.s  and additional stimulus from washington  these neighbors  say they want to be optimistic but the  uncertainty that lies ahead  is weighing on them looking into 2021  are you  concerned at all about your ability to  pay your bills and meet your obligations  if i have to work for jobs i've done it  before i'll do it again  but just anything i have to do to  keep the heat on the lights on and a  roof over my head  and food on the table i will i'll find a  job and do it

